ve)**: By playing J8, Black keeps the initiative, forcing White to respond, rather than playing passively.\n\nReason:\nBlack\'s horizontal line on row 8 is the most significant structure on the board. Since White blocks the left side at F8, Black must extend to the right at J8. This move creates a "Four" threat, forcing White to defend at K8. More importantly, it prevents White from playing J8, which would completely neutralize Black\'s attacking potential in that row. It is the only move that maintains offensive pressure and structural integrity for Black.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 8}'}
