ite\'s existing stone at G9. White could then play F9 to create a new Open 3 threat on Row 9.\n2.  **Blocking at E9:** If Black blocks at E9, this prevents the immediate win and also occupies the critical E9 square. By placing a stone here, Black disrupts White\'s potential to connect with G9 horizontally. Although White can still play at I5 to threaten a win at J4, blocking at E9 denies White the stronger positional advantage on the left side of the board and effectively neutralizes the secondary threat on Row 9.\n\nTherefore, E9 is the optimal defensive move.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "E", "row": 9}'}
