ent can only block one. I determined that creating a move with a double threat gives me a guaranteed win, if my opponent doesn\'t create a move with a double threat on their turn.\n\nI assessed whether there were any "forbidden" moves based on the type of Gomoku game I\'m playing. However, my move wasn\'t a forbidden move, even in the version of Gomoku with added constraints, so my plan remained.\n\nIn the end, I\'ve concluded that the move K10 is the best move. It\'s a double threat. It creates two "split 3s" which will be an immediate win. It\'s not on the occupied list, and it\'s a legal move. I\'m confident in my analysis. It\'s an executable plan.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Based on the analysis of the board state:\n\n**Board Survey:**\nBlack (X) has several clusters of stones, particularly around row 8 and columns H and I.\n- There is a blocked 4-in-a-row at Row 8 (G8-J8, blocked by F8 and K8).\n- There is a blocked 4-in-a-row at Column H (H7-H10, blocked by H6 and H11).\n- There is a blocked diagonal 4-in-a-row (J8-G11, blocked by K7 and F12).\nThese existing "fours" are neutralized and cannot become winning lines. Black must look for new opportunities.\n\n**Threat Assessment:**\nWhite (O) has scattered stones but no immediate lethal threats (no open 3s or 4s). The most relevant white group is around Row 7 (G7-K7, broken by H7) which is not a threat. Black is free to play an offensive move.\n\n**Candidate Move Analysis:**\nI searched for moves that create double threats (forks) to guarantee a win.\n- **Move K10:**\n    - **Diagonal Threat:** Forms a "split 3" pattern with existing stones H7 and I8 (Line: H7-I8-gap-K10). If Black follows up at J9, this becomes a connected diagonal line of 4 stones (H7-I8-J9-K10). The ends of this line (G6 and L11) are empty, so this would be an "Open 4" (an immediate win).\n    - **Horizontal Threat:** Forms a "split 3" pattern on Row 10 with existing stones H10 and I10 (Line: H10-I10-gap-K10). If Black follows up at J10, this becomes a connected horizontal line of 4 stones (H10-I10-J10-K10). The ends of this line (G10 and L10) are empty, so this would also be an "Open 4" (an immediate win).\n    \n    By playing K10, Black creates two intersection points (J9 and J10) where playing a stone results in an Open 4. Since these are two distinct squares and blocking one does not block the other\'s line, White cannot defend against both threats simultaneously.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nPlaying at K10 creates a double threat that leads to a guaranteed win. It is a legal move as the coordinate is not occupied.\n\nEvaluation: The move at K10 creates a powerful fork, setting up two separate "Open 4" threats on the next turn (one diagonal via J9, one horizontal via J10).\nReason: K10 connects with H7/I8 diagonally and H10/I10 horizontally. This move forces the opponent to block one of the potential Open 4 lines, leaving the other open for Black to complete and win.\nAnswer: {"col": "K", "row": 10}'}
